{"source":"We have the first version of the walking map ready, with 12 stops and short notes for each one. It took longer than I expected to make the notes sound like they belonged together. Some people sent memories, some sent dates, and we are trying to make room for both without turning every stop into a wall of text. The copy at the community center is still a draft, so please write comments on the sheet beside it rather than directly on the map.

Marta checked the route on Saturday and said the crossing by the bakery is harder than it looks on paper. She had to wait through two light changes before she felt comfortable crossing. I will redraw that section this week, and we may need to move the stop marker a little farther up the street. We are still aiming to print a small batch in November, assuming the council approves the final map.

Thanks to everyone who sent in stories about the old bridge. I have not managed to fit all of them into the short note, but they helped us choose what to point out along the route.","objective":"Update the group accurately: the map now has 14 stops, and the print target has moved to December. Keep the crossing issue and the council approval condition clear.","style":{"label":"Natural","description":"Plain, human rhythm. Keeps your register; removes the polish that makes prose sound manufactured.","register":"neutral","wordingRetention":"medium"},"localFindings":[],"discourseFindings":[],"sourceVoice":[],"protected":{"numbers":["12","2"],"dates":["november","saturday"],"names":["Marta"],"quotations":[],"links":[],"negations":2},"protectedPhrases":["on the map","into the short","along the route"]}

{"source":"The little free library has been busy since we moved it under the awning. Books are turning over much faster, and someone left a lovely stack of picture books last week. The lower shelf is mostly children's books now, which is nice to see, but it also means grown-up books are getting stacked on top. A few have been wedged in sideways and are hard to pull out without bringing the whole pile with them.

If you are dropping books off, please take a moment to stand them upright. You do not need to sort them carefully; a little space on the shelf is enough. On rainy days, close the door firmly, because it tends to catch on the frame. The books inside were dry after the last storm, but we would like to keep it that way.

We are planning a painting afternoon later this month to freshen up the outside. I'll share a date once the forecast looks sensible. If you have a small brush you do not mind lending, bring it along when the day comes.","objective":"Edit this for the neighborhood newsletter. Make the two requests easy to spot without sounding scolding.","style":{"label":"Natural","description":"Plain, human rhythm. Keeps your register; removes the polish that makes prose sound manufactured.","register":"neutral","wordingRetention":"medium"},"localFindings":[],"discourseFindings":[],"sourceVoice":[],"protected":{"numbers":[],"dates":[],"names":[],"quotations":[],"links":[],"negations":3},"protectedPhrases":["lower shelf","up books","on the shelf","after the last"]}

{"source":"For Saturday's soup exchange, please bring one container to share and one empty container to take something home. A small portion is fine; nobody needs to make a huge pot. Label your soup with its ingredients, especially nuts, dairy, and wheat. We will put blank cards and pens near the door for anyone who forgets a label. It helps people choose what to try without having to ask the same questions at every table.

We have a vegetarian table, but it isn't an allergen-free area, since everything is made in different home kitchens. Please do not assume a soup is safe for a particular allergy because of where it is sitting. If you would rather just come taste and talk, that is fine too. You do not need to bring soup to be welcome, and there should be enough for small samples.

The hall opens at noon, and we will start swapping around 12:30. We will have a few extra spoons, though bringing your own would help. The tables will stay up until 2 so people can arrive at a relaxed pace.","objective":"Make these instructions easier to follow for first-time visitors while staying friendly and accurate.","style":{"label":"Natural","description":"Plain, human rhythm. Keeps your register; removes the polish that makes prose sound manufactured.","register":"neutral","wordingRetention":"medium"},"localFindings":[{"name":"Uniform sentence rhythm","guidance":"Let some sentences be short and plain and others run longer, where the thought calls for it. Do not add or drop content to create variety.","examples":["For Saturday's soup exchange, please bring one container to share and one empty container…"]}],"discourseFindings":[],"sourceVoice":[],"protected":{"numbers":["12","30","2"],"dates":["saturday"],"names":[],"quotations":[],"links":[],"negations":5},"protectedPhrases":[]}

{"source":"Here is the Friday update on the reading room refresh. The new tables arrived on Wednesday, and the team assembled all eight yesterday. We also tested the adjustable lamps with three staff members who use the room regularly. Two asked for a warmer bulb, so we are trying a different option before placing the final lamp order.

The wall repair is still on track for next week. We have not set a reopening date because the paint needs to cure before the furniture can go back against that wall. The contractor expects to finish painting on Tuesday, but that timing depends on the repair drying as planned. I’ll confirm the date after the contractor’s inspection.

Thanks to everyone who worked around the closed room this week. If a visitor asks when it will reopen, please say that we will post the date once the wall work has passed inspection. I’ll send the next update on Wednesday even if the date is still pending.","objective":"Prepare a polished but restrained staff update. Keep the sequence, the feedback on the lamps, the contractor’s conditional estimate, and the reopening uncertainty intact.","style":{"label":"Natural","description":"Plain, human rhythm. Keeps your register; removes the polish that makes prose sound manufactured.","register":"neutral","wordingRetention":"medium"},"localFindings":[],"discourseFindings":[],"sourceVoice":[],"protected":{"numbers":["8","3","2"],"dates":["tuesday","wednesday","friday"],"names":["I’ll"],"quotations":[],"links":[],"negations":1},"protectedPhrases":["on Wednesday","on the repair","after the contractor’s","closed room"]}

