{"source":"The first round of neighborhood listening sessions is complete. We held three sessions in different parts of town and collected written comments from people who could not attend. The sessions were small, so the notes show what participants raised; they do not represent a survey of all residents.

The primary thematic throughline at this juncture is an emergent desire to optimize the community interface around scheduling visibility. In other words, people repeatedly asked where they could find an up-to-date schedule for shared rooms. Several said they had checked the printed notice board and found an older version.

We are testing a single online schedule and a dated printout at each entrance. The online page is ready for a staff review, but we have not chosen who will maintain the printouts. Once that owner is confirmed, we can try the approach for six weeks and ask visitors whether the information is easier to find. I’ll share a short proposal next Tuesday.","objective":"Rewrite the inflated opening of the second paragraph in ordinary language. Keep the other paragraphs close to the original and preserve the limits of the listening-session evidence and the unresolved printout owner.","style":{"label":"Natural","description":"Plain, human rhythm. Keeps your register; removes the polish that makes prose sound manufactured.","register":"neutral","wordingRetention":"medium"},"localFindings":[],"discourseFindings":[],"sourceVoice":[],"protected":{"numbers":["3","6"],"dates":["tuesday"],"names":["I’ll"],"quotations":[],"links":[],"negations":3},"protectedPhrases":[]}

{"source":"The nightly export writes one file per workspace. Each workspace's run begins at 2 a.m. in that workspace's time zone, so the files do not all appear at the same clock time for the team watching them. A large workspace can take more than an hour to finish. The start time is therefore not a promise that its file will be ready by 3 a.m.

If someone reports a missing file at 3 a.m., first identify the workspace and check its export status page. The page reports either completion or a specific error for each workspace. A run that has not yet produced a file should not be called a failure solely because it has passed the one-hour mark. This is especially relevant when comparing a small workspace with a much larger one.

Use the status shown for the affected workspace when deciding whether to file an incident. Include the workspace and the reported error if there is one, so the person investigating can start with the actual run rather than the absence of a file.","objective":"Make this internal operations note more concise and ordered by action. Preserve the timing, the one-file rule, and the warning against assuming failure at 3 a.m.","style":{"label":"Natural","description":"Plain, human rhythm. Keeps your register; removes the polish that makes prose sound manufactured.","register":"neutral","wordingRetention":"medium"},"localFindings":[],"discourseFindings":[],"sourceVoice":[],"protected":{"numbers":["2","3"],"dates":[],"names":["Use","Include"],"quotations":[],"links":[],"negations":4},"protectedPhrases":["larger one","Include the workspace"]}

{"source":"We built the small dial because the big dashboard was too much to glance at while mixing. Most of the time, you only want to know where the batch is now and where you asked it to stop. The dial shows the current temperature, and the pale mark shows that stopping point. It is a target you set, not a warning that the batch is in trouble.

The dial keeps those two pieces of information together without asking you to read a full chart while your hands are busy. It does not try to tell the whole story of the batch. If you want to see how the temperature changed over time, the dashboard still has the full temperature history.

That split is deliberate. The dial is for the quick look; the dashboard is for the longer look, if that's your thing. We want the mark to be useful at a glance, especially when it sits close to the current reading, without making it sound like an alarm.","objective":"Smooth this product copy while keeping its plainspoken, lightly playful voice. Make the target mark unambiguous without turning the paragraph into marketing copy.","style":{"label":"Natural","description":"Plain, human rhythm. Keeps your register; removes the polish that makes prose sound manufactured.","register":"neutral","wordingRetention":"medium"},"localFindings":[{"name":"Repeated sentence openings","guidance":"Vary how sentences begin by leading with what matters in each one, not by swapping synonyms.","examples":["The dial"]}],"discourseFindings":[],"sourceVoice":[],"protected":{"numbers":["2"],"dates":[],"names":[],"quotations":[],"links":[],"negations":4},"protectedPhrases":["without asking","over time"]}

{"source":"A token can be used for one active session at a time. If a second session starts with the same token, the first session ends within a few seconds. This can look like an unexplained disconnect on the first device, especially if the person there did not know the token was being used somewhere else. The session change is worth checking before treating the disconnect as a device fault.

We do not currently warn the first device before it is disconnected. By the time its user sees the session end, the second session may already be active. When troubleshooting, check whether anyone else started a session with that token around the same time. That question is often more useful than repeatedly reconnecting the first device.

If two people need to work at once, each person should sign in with a separate account rather than share a token. Reusing one token will continue to replace the earlier active session, even if both people are working on different devices.","objective":"Clarify this technical FAQ for users who are troubleshooting a disconnect. Preserve the one-session constraint and the lack of advance warning.","style":{"label":"Natural","description":"Plain, human rhythm. Keeps your register; removes the polish that makes prose sound manufactured.","register":"neutral","wordingRetention":"medium"},"localFindings":[],"discourseFindings":[],"sourceVoice":[],"protected":{"numbers":["2"],"dates":[],"names":[],"quotations":[],"links":[],"negations":2},"protectedPhrases":["first session","on the first","first device","earlier active"]}

{"source":"Draft note for the shipping page: the free-delivery threshold is changing from $40 to $50 on 15 June. The current page still describes the $40 threshold and uses it in its example, so both places need updating together. A reader should be able to see which threshold applies without having to infer it from an example that says something different.

Orders placed before 15 June keep the threshold shown at checkout when they were placed. The new threshold should not be described as a retroactive change to an existing order. The standard delivery fee remains $6 for orders below the applicable threshold. We are changing the amount that qualifies an order for free delivery, not the delivery fee itself.

Please use the new $50 threshold and the start date in the revised page text, and check that the example agrees with them. Keep the earlier-order explanation close enough to the new rule that a returning customer can find it. Do not imply that an order already placed will be recalculated.","objective":"Write the updated shipping-page explanation using the new $50 threshold and 15 June start date. Keep the treatment of earlier orders and the $6 fee accurate.","style":{"label":"Natural","description":"Plain, human rhythm. Keeps your register; removes the polish that makes prose sound manufactured.","register":"neutral","wordingRetention":"medium"},"localFindings":[],"discourseFindings":[],"sourceVoice":[],"protected":{"numbers":["$40","$50","$6"],"dates":["15 June","15 June"],"names":["Draft"],"quotations":[],"links":[],"negations":4},"protectedPhrases":["in its example","earlier-order"]}

{"source":"Before replacing the filter, switch off the fan and wait until the blades have stopped. Do this before opening the front panel, even if you only plan to check which filter is inside. The panel lifts from its lower edge and is held in place by magnets. There are no screws to remove, so the panel should not need to be taken apart.

Once the panel is open, slide the used filter straight out. Put the new filter in with its arrow facing the fan. Check the arrow before you push the filter into place; it is easier to correct its direction while the filter is still in your hands. The guide should make that orientation easy to spot, because the two sides can look similar at a quick glance.

Close the front panel before turning the fan back on. That is the last step, after the new filter is in place. Keeping the instructions in this order should help someone changing a filter for the first time without requiring them to guess which step comes next.","objective":"Polish these instructions for a printed quick-start guide. Keep every safety and orientation step in order.","style":{"label":"Natural","description":"Plain, human rhythm. Keeps your register; removes the polish that makes prose sound manufactured.","register":"neutral","wordingRetention":"medium"},"localFindings":[],"discourseFindings":[],"sourceVoice":[],"protected":{"numbers":["2"],"dates":[],"names":["Put","Close"],"quotations":[],"links":[],"negations":3},"protectedPhrases":["off the fan","before opening","inside. The panel","in place","with its arrow","into place","Close the front","last step","after the new"]}

{"source":"An empty timeline can make it look as though a project's tasks have disappeared. The timeline only shows work that has a date to place on it. If the project has no dated milestones, the timeline will be empty. Tasks without dates still appear in the list view; they have not been removed from the project. Start there if you need to confirm that the tasks are still present.

To put a task on the timeline, open the task and add a due date. Then return to the timeline to see whether it appears where you expect. This is the same task you saw in the list, not a new copy created for the timeline.

If milestones came from a spreadsheet, check their dates as well. A spreadsheet that used a different date format may have left dates needing correction after import. That is one possible cause of an empty timeline, not proof that the import failed or that every imported milestone is affected.","objective":"Make this support article clearer for someone who thinks their tasks vanished. Keep the difference between list view and timeline, and describe the import issue as a possibility.","style":{"label":"Natural","description":"Plain, human rhythm. Keeps your register; removes the polish that makes prose sound manufactured.","register":"neutral","wordingRetention":"medium"},"localFindings":[],"discourseFindings":[],"sourceVoice":[],"protected":{"numbers":[],"dates":[],"names":["Start"],"quotations":[],"links":[],"negations":5},"protectedPhrases":["without dates","in the list","on the timeline","open the task","left dates","after import"]}

{"source":"We've been calling this a launch in conversation, though it is really a small trial. Three library branches volunteered to try the checkout form with their staff. They will use it for six weeks and tell us where it slows them down. That feedback is the reason for starting with a small group: we need to see how the form fits ordinary desk work before asking more branches to use it.

After the trial, we will review what those staff report and decide what needs to change. We may find small wording issues, or we may find a step that takes longer than it should. At this point, we do not have those findings, so an announcement should not talk as if the form is ready everywhere.

Please describe the six-week period as a trial with the three volunteer branches. We can say that we will consider inviting more branches after reviewing feedback. I don't want to promise a citywide rollout yet, or make the volunteers sound like the first wave of a schedule we have already settled.","objective":"Rewrite this as a calm internal project update. Preserve the author's caution and the fact that wider rollout depends on feedback.","style":{"label":"Natural","description":"Plain, human rhythm. Keeps your register; removes the polish that makes prose sound manufactured.","register":"neutral","wordingRetention":"medium"},"localFindings":[],"discourseFindings":[],"sourceVoice":[],"protected":{"numbers":["3","6"],"dates":[],"names":["We've"],"quotations":[],"links":[],"negations":3},"protectedPhrases":["with their staff","with a small","more branches","After the trial","with the three","after reviewing"]}

{"source":"For general requests that arrive by 4 p.m., we usually send an initial reply on the same business day. Requests that arrive after 4 p.m. are normally handled the next business day. Those are typical response times, not a guarantee that every issue can be investigated or resolved on that schedule. The time a request arrives matters, but so does what the request is about.

An initial reply means that we have received the request and can begin working out what it needs. It is an acknowledgement, not a promise that the underlying issue will be fixed that day. Please keep that distinction in the FAQ so people know what to expect when they see a quick response from us.

If the service is unavailable, use the outage form instead of the general request form. The outage form is the path the on-call team watches for service interruptions. Someone facing an outage should not have to read through the routine response-time details before finding that instruction.","objective":"Improve this service FAQ without making the response-time language more absolute. Make the outage path easy to spot.","style":{"label":"Natural","description":"Plain, human rhythm. Keeps your register; removes the polish that makes prose sound manufactured.","register":"neutral","wordingRetention":"medium"},"localFindings":[],"discourseFindings":[],"sourceVoice":[],"protected":{"numbers":["4"],"dates":[],"names":["FAQ","Someone"],"quotations":[],"links":[],"negations":3},"protectedPhrases":[]}

{"source":"Project note: The map is almost done, and by almost I mean the north half is done. The paths and landmarks there have been checked against the latest park map. I still need to verify three footpaths in the south section with the parks office, because the public map shows one of them ending at a gate that may have moved. Don't mark the draft as final yet.

I sent the parks office the three path names and a screenshot on Monday. They usually answer within a few days, but there isn't a firm turnaround time. The route notes are otherwise in place, including the accessible entrances and the two places where the path surface changes. I haven't confirmed whether the small bridge is open, so that note is still marked for review. I also need to check that the printed legend uses the same names as the signs at the trail junctions.

If nobody gets back to me by Friday, I'll publish the version with those paths marked unconfirmed. I'll include a short note explaining that the southern paths still need a site check, rather than implying the map is complete. If they reply after that, I can update the file and date the revision. For now, please use the draft for comments only and send any corrections my way before it goes anywhere public. I can make the accessible entrance notes visible in the next draft while the three path confirmations remain pending. That keeps the portions we have checked useful without hiding the open questions.","objective":"Make the status and next step easy to follow without overstating completion or removing the fallback plan.","style":{"label":"Natural","description":"Plain, human rhythm. Keeps your register; removes the polish that makes prose sound manufactured.","register":"neutral","wordingRetention":"medium"},"localFindings":[],"discourseFindings":[],"sourceVoice":[],"protected":{"numbers":["3","2"],"dates":["monday","friday"],"names":["Project","The","Don't"],"quotations":[],"links":[],"negations":5},"protectedPhrases":["with the parks"]}

{"source":"Things for the workshop: I think we need about 18 chairs; last time we had 16 and borrowed two from the front room. Please check with the building desk before moving any this time, since another group may be using that space. The extension cords are in the cabinet, but check that they still work and that the longer one is there. I haven't tested them since we packed up in April. If the longer cord is missing, tell me before buying a replacement; there may be another one in storage.

Ask Niko if the speaker is coming. If not, use mine, though I'll need to bring it in before the room opens. We should print the handouts only after we settle the room layout, because the current diagram puts the chairs in a circle and that may not leave a clear route to the door. The side room seems like the best option for the activity, but I think it's booked.

The calendar was behaving oddly yesterday: it showed the side room as reserved, then the entry disappeared when I refreshed. I'll ask at the desk rather than assume it's free. Once we know which room we're using, we can confirm the chair count and print the right number of copies. No need to order extra supplies until those two details are settled. I'll update the shared note after I hear back so nobody sets up from the old diagram by mistake. The room and equipment decisions can then be confirmed in one place before setup begins.","objective":"Organize this note so the confirmed tasks, open questions, and dependencies are distinct, keeping all uncertainty intact.","style":{"label":"Natural","description":"Plain, human rhythm. Keeps your register; removes the polish that makes prose sound manufactured.","register":"neutral","wordingRetention":"medium"},"localFindings":[],"discourseFindings":[],"sourceVoice":[],"protected":{"numbers":["18","16","2"],"dates":["april"],"names":["Things","Ask Niko"],"quotations":[],"links":[],"negations":5},"protectedPhrases":["last time","in before","before the room","in one"]}

{"source":"At the last check-in, we agreed to print 80 copies for the first session and hold 20 back for the second. That leaves a small reserve in case a few people arrive without registering, but it isn't a general extra run. The first session is the larger one, so please keep those copies in a separate box and label it before delivery. If the printer packages them together, ask for a clear label on the outside rather than opening every bundle at the venue.

Jo has the final file and plans to send it to the print shop Wednesday morning. Before then, Jo will confirm that the page numbers and the large-print version are both included. We don't need to change the wording unless someone spots a factual error; if that happens, flag it before the file is sent so the printer doesn't charge us for a corrected batch. Please keep the working file in the shared project folder so the group can find the approved version later.

If the quote is over $95, pause and bring it back to the group; we've only budgeted $120 total, including the second run. The $95 figure is the point where we need to discuss options, not permission to spend the full allocation on the first batch. Once the quote is approved, please send the receipt and note how many copies were delivered. We'll decide whether to use the held-back 20 after we see how many people attend the first session. The second run should use the same approved file unless the group agrees to a correction.","objective":"Make the quantities, owner, timing, approval threshold, and total budget easy to distinguish. Preserve the condition exactly.","style":{"label":"Natural","description":"Plain, human rhythm. Keeps your register; removes the polish that makes prose sound manufactured.","register":"neutral","wordingRetention":"medium"},"localFindings":[{"name":"Uniform sentence rhythm","guidance":"Let some sentences be short and plain and others run longer, where the thought calls for it. Do not add or drop content to create variety.","examples":["At the last check-in, we agreed to print 80 copies for the first session and hold 20 back…"]}],"discourseFindings":[],"sourceVoice":[],"protected":{"numbers":["80","20","$95","$120"],"dates":["wednesday"],"names":["Jo","We'll"],"quotations":[],"links":[],"negations":5},"protectedPhrases":["first session","before delivery","before the file","approved version","on the first","first batch","approved, please","approved file"]}

{"source":"The lantern walk starts at 7 p.m. at Maple Park's south gate. Please arrive a few minutes early so we can hand out the loaner lanterns and start together. We'll follow the paved loop and finish by the picnic shelter, where tea will be available. The route is about a mile and takes us past the pond before it circles back toward the shelter. There are no scheduled stops, but anyone can walk at their own pace. Volunteers will wait at the turns so nobody has to guess which way the loop goes.

Bring a lantern if you have one; we have 15 to lend. We can't promise a loaner for everyone who arrives without one, so if you borrow one, please return it to the volunteers at the shelter. Battery lanterns are easiest to carry, though a small hand-held light is fine too. Please avoid open flames on the route. If you bring a stroller, the paved loop is the simplest route, but the path narrows near the pond and people may need to pass single file.

The event will move to the gym if the weather is severe. We'll post the decision on the community board by 4 p.m. that afternoon, and the walk won't go ahead outdoors during lightning or high winds. The park path is not lit, so children should stay with an adult. The paved surface is mostly even, but there are a couple of narrow spots near the pond; volunteers will be at the turns to help people stay on the loop. If you need to leave early, tell one of them before heading back to the gate. Tea will be served at the finish either way, and the board notice will include the gym entrance if we move indoors.","objective":"Make the event details welcoming and scannable while preserving the route, limited loaners, weather plan, and supervision note.","style":{"label":"Natural","description":"Plain, human rhythm. Keeps your register; removes the polish that makes prose sound manufactured.","register":"neutral","wordingRetention":"medium"},"localFindings":[],"discourseFindings":[],"sourceVoice":[],"protected":{"numbers":["7","15","4"],"dates":[],"names":["Maple Park","We'll","Battery"],"quotations":[],"links":[],"negations":6},"protectedPhrases":["south gate","out the loaner","without one","on the route","on the loop","include the gym"]}
